From: tt-bh-linux <noreply@tenstorrent.com>
Subject: [PATCH] include/configs/qemu-riscv: SDRAM base = 0x400030000000

`setup_dest_addr` in common/board_f.c unconditionally overrides
gd->ram_base with CFG_SYS_SDRAM_BASE, immediately clobbering whatever
dram_init read from the DTB. The qemu-riscv config defaults this to
0x80000000 (qemu-virt's RAM base) — wrong for tt-bh-linux, where
L2CPU DRAM starts at 0x400030000000. The wrong base puts
relocaddr/ram_top in the 0x180000000 range, malloc allocations land
there too, and virtqueues set up by U-Boot are outside the actual
DRAM region the daemon mmaps. Result: daemon-side virtio worker
panics with "virtqueue desc address X below L2CPU memory start".

Bumping the #define to our DRAM base keeps qemu-riscv64 builds for
real qemu broken (since it expects 0x80000000), but the qemu-riscv
config is the upstream we use — tt-bh-linux ships its own U-Boot via
this build tree, not a stock qemu-riscv image. If we ever need both
targets the fix is a runtime check or a per-board config; for now,
hardcoded is fine.

See tt-bh-linux #49.

---
--- a/include/configs/qemu-riscv.h
+++ b/include/configs/qemu-riscv.h
@@ -8,7 +8,17 @@
 
 #include <linux/sizes.h>
 
-#define CFG_SYS_SDRAM_BASE		0x80000000
+/*
+ * tt-bh: SDRAM base address. setup_dest_addr in common/board_f.c
+ * unconditionally overrides gd->ram_base with this value, clobbering
+ * whatever dram_init (via fdtdec) reads from the DTB. Patching the
+ * #define here is the only way to make the relocaddr/ram_top math
+ * land inside our actual L2CPU DRAM. Real qemu-riscv64 doesn't share
+ * this address; if the daemon ever needs to support multiple
+ * platforms the fix is a runtime check or per-board config. See
+ * tt-bh-linux #49.
+ */
+#define CFG_SYS_SDRAM_BASE		0x400030000000UL
 
 #define RISCV_MMODE_TIMERBASE		0x2000000
 #define RISCV_MMODE_TIMEROFF		0xbff8
